{
  "_comment": "Named moves (D30, creature progression). Every species in data/creatures/species.json points at exactly one quick and one charged id here through its own `moves` field. `type` is flavour/vocabulary (ground|water|air) and is not cross-checked against the wielding species' own type -- Reedwing borrows an air-flavoured charged move on purpose, because the wild canon calls it a Water/Air waterfowl.",
  "_comment_balance": "Each move now carries its OWN reach, arc, timing, commitment and presentation. Before this every one of the 26 was power 1.0 with the identical range and cone, so a named move was a display string and nothing else and all quick attacks felt the same as each other -- the owner's report was that TMs 'aren't upgrades', which is what happens when the thing being upgraded has no stats. `power` stays a multiplier over combat.json's player_quick/player_charged blocks; `range`, `cone_degrees`, `windup`, `recovery` and `lunge` OVERRIDE the matching key in those blocks (combat_manager.gd::_move_profile). Ranged moves trade arc for distance and give up the lunge entirely -- you plant and fire instead of closing. ALL TUNABLE.",
  "_comment_slots": "`slot` is \"quick\" (fast, low-commitment, builds energy) or \"charged\" (the energy-spend finisher). Quick moves carry `energy_gain`; charged moves carry `energy_cost`, mirroring combat.json's energy.gain_per_quick / energy.charged_cost so a designer tuning one move at a time does not have to touch two files to keep them honest.",
